[Response A]
Overfitting is when a model learns too much from the training data and doesn't generalize well to new, unseen data. It happens because the model becomes too complex and starts memorizing the patterns it saw during training, rather than actually predicting what will happen in real-world situations. This can lead to poor performance on new data and make it difficult for the model to learn from other similar patterns. Overfitting can be avoided by using simpler models or techniques that focus more on understanding the underlying patterns of the data.

